namespace gismo
{

/** \defgroup gsALMSolvers Arc-length methods
\ingroup gsStructuralAnalysis

Performs the arc length method to solve a nonlinear equation system.

More details can be found in [1].

The classes of this group are \ref gsALMBase (the base class, with the singular-point
detection, the extended-system singular-point solve and the branch switch), its methods
\ref gsALMLoadControl, \ref gsALMRiks, \ref gsALMCrisfield and
\ref gsALMConsistentCrisfield, the parallel \ref gsAPALM, and the landscape-exploration
pair \ref gsALMExploration and \ref gsALMLandscape described below. Singular-point
localization and classification are documented in \ref gsALMSolvers_SingularPoints; the
two branch-switch mechanisms (the true emanating tangent, and its fallback) in
\ref gsALMSolvers_BranchSwitching.

\section gsALMSolvers_Exploration Landscape exploration

A single arc-length run follows one equilibrium curve. Obtaining a whole bifurcation
diagram means driving that solver in a loop -- step, stability check, singular-point
computation, branch switch -- and book-keeping the curves it produces.
\ref gsALMExploration automates exactly that loop on top of a caller-configured
\ref gsALMBase solver: starting from one (or several) converged seed states it works off a
job queue, classifies each detected crossing as a turning (limit) point or a branch point,
queues one branch-switch job per critical-mode sign, de-duplicates jobs and retraced
sweeps, and assembles the result in a \ref gsALMLandscape. It is library code: it carries
no shell or elasticity dependency, everything problem-specific enters through the solver
and an optional callback mapping a free-DOF vector to a deformed geometry.

Four properties govern how it is configured:
- <b>One job produces one curve, traced in BOTH arc-length directions.</b> A subcritical
  branch lies on the other side of the critical load and the side cannot be read off the
  critical mode, so both directions are swept -- into a SINGLE curve object, running from
  one far end through the start state to the other. The two halves of a seed, or of a
  branch, can therefore never be duplicates of each other.
- <b>Three independent comparison tolerances, with three different scopes.</b>
  \c DedupTol (default \f$10^{-4}\f$) de-duplicates branch JOBS; \c MoveTol (default
  \f$10^{-6}\f$) is the no-progress guard rejecting a stalled step; \c RetraceTol (default
  \f$8\cdot10^{-2}\f$) decides whether a SWEEP is retracing an already-stored curve. The
  last is deliberately the loosest: it compares samplings of two curves which, on different
  arc-length grids, never coincide. It is not compared directly either -- the test fires
  once, when the sweep has travelled \c StartSteps*SwitchLength from the branch point, and
  a genuinely emanating branch has by then departed from its parent only as the square root
  of that distance, so the tolerance is scaled by
  \f$\sqrt{\texttt{StartSteps}\cdot\texttt{SwitchLength}/\texttt{Length}}\f$ and the
  criterion is the same one at every arc length.
- <b>A retracing sweep is rewound, not the curve.</b> Of the two directions swept at a
  singular point, the one pointing away from the emanating branch falls back onto the
  parent; since it is a sweep and not a curve, rewinding it leaves the genuine half found
  by the other sweep intact.
- <b>\c MaxPointsPerCurve is a per-SWEEP budget.</b> A curve traced in both directions runs
  it once per direction and may hold up to twice that many accepted points, plus the
  singular points stored on it, which the budget never counted.

<b>Arc-length control: halve-on-failure by default, opt-in growth.</b> The shipped control
is halve-and-snap-back: a failed step halves the arc length, and the next accepted step
snaps back to the sweep's base length (\c Length on a seed curve, \c SwitchLength on a
branch curve). \c StepGrowth (default \c false, opt-in) replaces the snap-back with a
pde2path-style (\c sscontrol.m) doubling ladder: an accepted step whose corrector converged
within \c StepGrowthIter iterations doubles the arc length instead, clamped in MAGNITUDE at
the base length, so with \c StepGrowth on the arc length can only APPROACH what the default
control already uses, more gradually, after a halving -- it never exceeds it. This makes
\c StepGrowth strictly MORE conservative than the default, not faster: it trades a single
snap-back for a multi-step ramp, so read it as a smoother RECOVERY, not as an acceleration
of a slow default (the default recovery is not slow -- it is immediate).
\c StepGrowthIter (default \c -1, meaning half the solver's own \c MaxIter) sets the
iteration-count threshold below which a step counts as "grew"; \c -1 follows pde2path's own
convention \c dsinciter \c = \c imax/2 (\c stanparam.m), because this tree's \c MaxIter is a
generous cap (default 100), not a target iteration count, and has no direct counterpart of
pde2path's \c imax in this library.

<b>Localizing a singular point.</b> \c BisecMax and \c BisecLengthFloor control the
bisection that localizes a detected inertia crossing before it is classified; see
\ref gsALMSolvers_SingularPoints for the whole pipeline. \c RetraceBallFloor (default
\c 0, disabled) is a related but separate knob: it floors the FALLBACK-NUDGE branch job's
absolute escape magnitude \f$1/\f$\c Perturbation against a multiple of the retrace-ball
radius at the branch point, because that radius scales with the solution norm while the
nudge magnitude does not (see \ref gsALMSolvers_BranchSwitching for the full rationale and
its per-driver calibration in \ref example_ModifiedBratuExploration.cpp).

Not every point of a landscape is equally certified. A converged curve point, a limit
point, and a converged extended-system singular-point solve (status \c Success) are all
stored with \c gsALMLandscape::Point::equilibrium \c == \c true: each met its OWN
termination test, regardless of how strict that test was. The solver option
\c SingularPointComposite (default \c false) is what decides how strict a converged
extended solve's own test was -- with it off, only \f$\|K_T V\|\to0\f$ was required
(the point is certified singular, not certified to be an equilibrium); with it on, the
equilibrium residuals \c TolF/TolU were required too. That choice is a RUN-LEVEL property
of the caller's own \c SingularPointComposite setting and is not re-encoded per row, so a
consumer that needs the stronger certificate must track its own setting rather than read a
per-point flag. A singular-point solve that did NOT converge is a different case entirely:
its traced, post-crossing point is stored with \c equilibrium \c == \c false AND the
companion flag \c gsALMLandscape::Point::unresolved \c == \c true (set atomically by
\c markUnresolvedSingular) -- a crossing was DETECTED but never refined, no \f$(U^*,L^*)\f$
exists, and no branch was followed. Such a point keeps its traced \c stability at whatever
\f$\pm1\f$ the tangent inertia gave it there (a genuine, converged equilibrium whose inertia
WAS measured) rather than being reset to \c 0, which is exactly what makes \c stability
usable as part of the certified-point filter below.

\note A consumer written against an OLDER version of this library that filtered
\c equilibrium \c == \c false to select genuine equilibria is now selecting the WRONG rows:
that filter's polarity was a defect and has been corrected. The replacement selectors, in order
of what they certify:
- \c isBifurcation \c && \c stability \c == \c 0 -- a point produced by a converged,
  certified extended-system solve (whether or not \c SingularPointComposite made that solve
  the stricter composite test -- check that option separately if the distinction matters);
- \c SingularPointComposite (read from the run, not the row) -- whether the RUN required the
  stricter composite termination test for every such point;
- \c unresolved \c == \c true -- a singular point that was detected but NOT refined; its
  location is only the traced, post-crossing point, and it must never be treated as a
  certified bifurcation.

A consumer that only needs the location of a crossing (plots, connectivity) may use every
point regardless of these flags.

The landscape CSV written by \c writeCsv has the header
<tt>curve,point,L,normU,stability,negatives,isBifurcation,parentCurve,parentPointIdx,equilibrium,unresolved</tt>
(column order matters to consumers). \c negatives is the tangent inertia count -- the
number of negative eigenvalues/pivots of \f$K_T\f$ that drove detection -- with \c -1
meaning "not recorded"; storing the count, rather than collapsing it to the \f$\pm1\f$ of
\c stability, is what lets a reader distinguish a \f$0\to1\f$ crossing from a
\f$1\to2\f$ secondary one. \c examples/plot_landscape.py parses this file KEYED ON THE
HEADER NAMES, never on column position, so the column order above is informative, not a
positional contract a consumer must match exactly.

\ref gsALMLandscape is a pure container plus writers: setting the explorer's
\c OutputPrefix rewrites <tt>&lt;prefix&gt;.csv</tt> -- and, in \c gsHDF5 -enabled builds,
<tt>&lt;prefix&gt;.h5</tt> -- after every completed curve, as a crash checkpoint;
\c writeParaview and \c saveHDF5 / \c loadHDF5 are available directly. The companion
script \c examples/plot_landscape.py renders such a CSV as a
\f$\lambda\f$--\f$\|\mathbf{u}\|\f$ equilibrium diagram (stable/unstable line styles,
bifurcation and fold markers, parent-child links); it needs NumPy and Matplotlib, and its
HDF5-based modes -- which reclassify points by their solution profile instead of by norm --
additionally need \c h5py and the <tt>.h5</tt> sibling of the CSV.

<b>The fold test function.</b> \a gsALMBase::foldTestFunction() returns AUTO-07p's fold
test function (\c toolboxae.f90), computed from the SAME already-factorized tangent a
stability evaluation forms, as one extra back-substitution against the (frozen) step
forcing \f$f\f$, bordered with \f$(w,\gamma)=(f,0)\f$: this bordering choice is OURS, not
AUTO's. The MAGNITUDE of the resulting scalar is bordering-independent, so any choice of
\f$(w,\gamma)\f$ would do on that count; what \f$(w,\gamma)=(f,0)\f$ fixes is the SIGN,
which is exactly the fold-vs-branch discriminator the test needs
(\f$\mathrm{sign}(f^TK_T^{-1}f)\f$) -- see \ref gsALMBase::foldTestFunction for the
derivation.
\note foldTestFunction() is an INSTRUMENT ONLY. Nothing in this library reads it for
singular-point DETECTION -- that remains the tangent inertia count \a negatives(), read
through the classification pipeline of \ref gsALMSolvers_SingularPoints. It returns NaN
whenever no value is available (before the first stability evaluation, after a
jacobian=false evaluation, or when the back-substitution itself fails); check with
\c math::isnan before using it.

Examples with the use of landscape exploration are
- \ref example_BratuExploration.cpp
- \ref example_ModifiedBratuExploration.cpp

and \ref example_ShearExploration.cpp runs the equivalent loop by hand on a shell problem.

\section gsALMSolvers_SingularPoints Singular points: localization, classification

A crossing is never classified at the raw, coarse-grid point where it was detected.
\ref gsALMExploration's per-sweep loop instead runs a LOCALIZE-FIRST pipeline, following
pde2path's \c bifdetec.m shape: (1) DETECT an inertia flip between two consecutive accepted
points (the tangent inertia, not the stability sign, so a \f$0\to1\f$ and a \f$1\to2\f$
crossing are both caught); (2) BISECT the bracketed arc-length interval on that same
inertia, capped by \c BisecMax bisection probes or terminated once the bracket width drops
below \c BisecLengthFloor -- a FRACTION of the sweep's own base arc length (\c Length on a
seed curve, \c SwitchLength on a branch curve), so the floor is relative, not absolute;
whichever bound is hit first stops the bisection, and exhausting either is a NORMAL
termination, not a failure (only a bracket no single probe ever refined counts as a failed
localization); (3) CLASSIFY at the localized, pre-crossing-side point of the final bracket,
with a FRESHLY assembled tangent (not the stale one from the coarse-grid point); (4) run the
extended (bordered-Newton) singular-point solve from there. This ordering -- localize before
classifying, with a fresh tangent -- follows both reference codes: pde2path bisects the
detection interval to convergence before classifying, and AUTO-07p folds localization and
classification into one convergence loop; the design this pipeline follows is closest to
pde2path's.

<b>The critical-mode tolerances, and why there are two.</b> Classifying a crossing needs
the critical mode \f$\phi\f$ (or \f$V\f$) of the LOCALIZED tangent, obtained by up to
\c SingularPointTestIt inverse-power sweeps (each
sweep is cheap -- ONE back-substitution on the already-factorized tangent, so raising it
costs little). \c SingularPointModeTol is the CONVERGENCE tolerance on that mode's own
direction (the sweep stops once the direction changes by less than this, up to sign,
between two sweeps), and it is DELIBERATELY DECOUPLED from \c SingularPointTestTol, the
verdict threshold the classification itself is judged against: the verdict threshold must
sit ABOVE the resolution at which the mode is computed, or the classification is read below
its own noise floor. \c SingularPointModeTol's default is the sentinel \c -1, resolved (in
\c getOptions()) to \c SingularPointTestTol*1e-2 whenever left at or below zero -- so
raising or lowering \c SingularPointTestTol also moves \c SingularPointModeTol's derived
target unless a caller pins the latter explicitly; a reader who changes one of the two
should check the other. An achieved direction error is published through
\c criticalModeError() and read against three bands: below \c SingularPointModeTol
("resolved", classify normally); at or above it but below \c SingularPointTestTol
("imprecise", classify normally, silently -- the verdict cannot change below its own
threshold); at or above \c SingularPointTestTol ("unresolved" -- the classification REFUSES
a verdict rather than certify one evaluated at or below the mode's own resolution).

\c SingularPointTestTol itself is what the classification thresholds: the cosine
\f$|\psi\cdot f|/\|f\|\f$ of the critical mode against the (frozen, per-step) forcing
\f$f\f$. It is the LEFT null vector \f$\psi\f$ of the tangent (the
right null vector of \f$K_T^{\mathsf T}\f$), which is the mathematically correct quantity
under a follower (state-dependent) load, and is numerically identical to the RIGHT null
vector \f$V\f$ on the symmetric default \c SimplicialLDLT solver path (no extra
factorization is performed there). \c SingularPointTestTol has a SECOND consumer beyond
classification: \a gsALMCrisfield's \c BorderedMode-driven degenerate-chart handling reads
the same cosine (there called \c modeCos, from \a gsALMCrisfield::_chartSingularityProbe,
the same limit-vs-branch statistic of Wriggers & Simo 1990) against this option to decide
between a chart-artifact re-border and a genuine bifurcation report -- a path only reachable
once \c BorderedMode makes the bordered chart runtime-selectable (see below). A caller
tuning \c SingularPointTestTol for the classification above should be aware it also governs
that second, bordered-chart-only decision.

<b>Provisional: the \c SingularPointTestTol default.</b> The default (\c 1e-4) is calibrated
BY MEASUREMENT (see \c tasks/11-testtol-table.md) against 2-DOF unit fixtures and 1-D Bratu
continuation drivers ONLY. Its behaviour at PDE scale -- large 2-D/3-D shell or solid
problems -- is UNEXAMINED: this is an open question, not a claim that the default is either
known good or known bad there. A caller running such a problem should treat the default as
provisional and cross-check any limit-vs-branch verdict against an independent signal, e.g.
the tangent inertia count exposed by \c negatives() (the \c negatives column of the landscape
CSV, described above).

<b>The bordered-solve corrector (\c BorderedMode).</b> \ref gsALMCrisfield exposes, at
runtime, which corrector chart \a gsALMCrisfield::step() uses: \c "Off" (default) never
touches the bordered chart and is bit-neutral by construction; \c "Fallback" attempts the
usual elimination chart first and retries once with the bordered chart on failure;
\c "Primary" attempts the bordered chart FIRST -- the references' own practice, both AUTO
and pde2path use the bordered (n+1) solve as the corrector rather than a fallback -- and
retries once with the elimination. The retry gate is
\c (status \c == \c NotConverged \c || \c status \c == \c SolverError): deliberately WIDER
than \c NotConverged alone, because a failed factorization at a near-singular
corrector-iterate tangent is exactly the case the bordered chart exists for. A failed retry
leaves no trace: \c m_DeltaUold, \c m_DeltaLold, \c m_arcLength, \c m_arcLength_prev,
\c m_stepTaken, \c m_stabilityPrev, \c m_phi, and the four stability-bookkeeping members
\c m_negatives, \c m_indicator, \c m_stabilityVec and \c m_stability are all snapshotted
before the retry and restored on failure -- the fourth member is required because
stability is DERIVED from the indicator in the very call a failed retry also runs, so
restoring the other three without it would leave the pair mutually inconsistent. The
reported status is clamped to attempt 1's own status, so a retry can never be reported as
WORSE than the attempt it replaces. The old switch \c BorderedFallback is a DEPRECATED
ALIAS: left at its default \c false it does nothing; set \c true while \c BorderedMode is
still at its own default \c "Off", it is mapped onto \c BorderedMode="Fallback" with a
once-per-solver-object warning -- an explicitly non-\c "Off" \c BorderedMode always wins
over the alias. \c BorderedSolver (default \c "LU") selects the sparse solver used for the
bordered (n+1)x(n+1) system; it must pivot.

\section gsALMSolvers_BranchSwitching Branch switching

Two mechanisms exist for stepping onto an emanating branch at a detected branch point, and
they are not peers: one is the primary mechanism, the other its fallback.

<b>The mechanism: \a gsALMBase::computeBranchTangent().</b> This computes the TRUE
emanating branch tangent \f$\tau_1\f$ at a simple (multiplicity-1) branch point, by the
algebraic branching equation (ABE) of pde2path's \c swibra. Its signature is
\code{.cpp}
virtual typename branchTangent::type
computeBranchTangent(const gsVector<T> & Ustar, const T & Lstar,
                     const gsVector<T> & phi1,
                     const gsVector<T> & tangentU0, const T & tangentL0,
                     gsVector<T> & tau1U, T & tau1L);
\endcode
taking the branch point \f$(U^*,L^*)\f$, the right critical mode \f$\phi_1\f$ there
(passed explicitly, e.g. \c solutionV(), so the contract carries no hidden ordering
requirement), and the incoming pre-crossing tangent/secant, and writing the emanating
tangent into \c tau1U/tau1L. It leaves \c m_U, \c m_L, \c m_V, \c m_jacMat and \c m_Vleft
exactly as it found them and performs two fresh Jacobian assemblies (never through
\c computeJacobian(), which would abort on the branch point's singular tangent). The
outcome is one of \c branchTangent::type: \c Success (\f$\tau_1\f$ is the emanating
tangent), \c TrivialBranch (\f$K_T\f$ does not vary along \f$\phi_1\f$ -- a linear problem
-- so \f$\tau_1=[\phi_1;0]\f$), \c ModeUnresolved (no usable left mode, or the
biorthogonalisation cosine \f$|\phi_1\cdot\psi_1|\f$ below \c BranchTangentTol),
\c Degenerate (the ABE's own leading coefficient is \f$\approx0\f$, "no distinct branch",
or the incoming tangent's load share vanishes), or \c AssemblyFailed. \c Success and
\c TrivialBranch are routed IDENTICALLY downstream by the explorer's branch-job creation
(both give a usable predictor tangent); a caller distinguishing them for diagnostic purposes
only should read the \c Verbose line that names the outcome, since it is the only place the
distinction survives.

\c BranchTangentPerturbation (default \f$10^{-3}\f$, called \f$\mathrm{del}\f$ in the ABE
literature) is the finite-difference perturbation used to difference \f$K_T\f$ along
\f$\phi_1\f$; pde2path's own warning applies unchanged -- do not choose \c del too small,
the two \f$K_T\f$ assemblies are differenced, and a \c del below the assembler's own noise
floor returns noise, not signal. \c BranchTangentTol (default \f$10^{-2}\f$, i.e.
\f$10\times\f$ \c BranchTangentPerturbation) is the RELATIVE floor below which an ABE
quantity counts as zero, for the same reason \c SingularPointModeTol sits below
\c SingularPointTestTol: the degeneracy verdict must sit ABOVE the \f$O(\mathrm{del})\f$
resolution of the finite-differenced coefficients it judges, not at or below it. Two
accuracy facts worth calibrating against honestly: the emanating tangent is a
finite-difference construction, not an exact one, and is accurate only to the resolution
that \c BranchTangentPerturbation sets; and, because \f$\phi_1\f$ is itself a null vector
determined only up to an overall sign by the inverse-power iteration that produced it,
\f$\tau_1\f$ inherits that same sign ambiguity -- a caller must not assume a particular
sign of the returned tangent.

<b>The fallback: \a gsALMBase::switchBranch() and \c Perturbation.</b> When
\c computeBranchTangent() does not return \c Success or \c TrivialBranch -- i.e. whenever
the true ABE emanating tangent cannot be resolved -- the explorer falls back to nudging the
singular point along the unit critical mode, \f$U \leftarrow U^*+V/\tau\f$, where
\f$\tau\f$ is the option \c Perturbation (default \f$10^3\f$). This fallback is NOT
vestigial: it is a live, load-bearing path taken whenever the ABE tangent path does not
resolve, which is a real and reachable outcome of \c computeBranchTangent(), not merely a
theoretical one.

\c Perturbation has THREE independent consumers, not two: (1) \a gsALMBase::switchBranch()
itself, the canonical fallback nudge; (2) \a gsALMExploration's own branch-job construction,
which -- for the FALLBACK-NUDGE path -- independently REPLICATES the \f$U^*+V/\tau\f$
formula rather than calling \c switchBranch(), a duplication that is itself a maintenance
hazard, since the replicated formula can silently diverge from \c switchBranch() if either
is edited alone; and (3) \c BranchEscape's default resolution on the TANGENT path (below).
\c BranchEscape (default \c -1, an explorer option) sets the Euler-predictor escape
magnitude used to step off a resolved ABE tangent: \c -1 reuses the UNFLOORED fallback-nudge
scale \f$1/\f$\c Perturbation directly from the solver's own options (consumer 3 above, and
NOT the floored value \c RetraceBallFloor may have applied to the fallback path -- the two
paths can therefore disagree in magnitude even at \c BranchEscape's default); any positive
value is used directly instead, and the option is inert on the fallback-nudge path itself.
\c BranchEscape exists because, near a pitchfork, the emanating branch leaves with
\f$\dot\lambda=0\f$ and grows like \f$C\sqrt{s}\f$ in arc length \f$s\f$, so a numerically
sound escape offset scales like \f$O(\sqrt{ds})\f$, not \f$O(ds)\f$ -- tying the escape
magnitude to the arc length instead would drop the emanating branch entirely.

\c RetraceBallFloor (default \c 0, disabled, so the shipped default is bit-identical to not
having the option at all) floors the fallback-nudge branch job's absolute magnitude
\f$1/\f$\c Perturbation against a multiple (margin) of the retrace-safety-net's own ball
radius at the branch point. It exists because that ball radius scales with
\f$\max(1,\|U^*\|)\f$ while the nudge magnitude \f$1/\f$\c Perturbation is ABSOLUTE: a
magnitude tuned to clear the ball at a low-state-norm locus can lose that margin entirely at
a high-state-norm one. There is no library-derived default -- \ref
example_ModifiedBratuExploration.cpp sets it explicitly, to a value derived as an equality
at that driver's own calibration locus (see its page for the value); this is a PER-DRIVER
calibration, not a library-wide fix, and a caller with their own high-state-norm problem
must derive and set their own margin.

